 "notes": [
  "Fluss 1.0's tablet server fails an upsert batch whose changelog passes 131,072 records (IndexOutOfBoundsException in ChangeTypeVectorWriter.writeChangeType, through ArrowWalBuilder.append): an update writes two records, and the client's default 2 MB batches of these small rows hold more. Every row's handle in a refused batch fails, and flush() does not raise, so with the handles left alone 1,881 and 1,151 of 2,000 lookups found the old city. The bench writes upserts through a connection with 512 KB batches (writer_batch_size, writer_dynamic_batch_size_min 128 KB) and awaits a handle every 1,000 rows after the flush: every lookup right in all three runs. Awaiting every handle instead took 321 s for the 2M upserts.",
  "Fluss's lookups wait for its client's 100 ms batch timeout, which Java's client can lower and Python's can't; its lookups per second are 64 lookupers' batches. Pondra's lookups are 64 HTTP connections from Python threads: what they show is the client's pace as much as the node's (0.14 ms a lookup measured on the node in serve_bench.py).",
  "Fluss's log scanner waits 5 ms at most per fetch here; at its default of 500 ms a row was seen about 340 ms after its write. Its tablet server needs server.data-disk.write-limit-ratio: 1.0 on this VM's disk.",
  "Fluss has no SQL of its own (that is Flink's): its group is the client reading the two columns and pyarrow grouping them; Pondra's is SQL on the node.",
  "Pondra's appends, acks and lookups go through its Python client, which keeps one connection a node and thread open since this run (urllib opened one a request). main (a61c05b, its own client) beside it, alternating: appends 4.10 M rows/s (this run 4.56 M), lookups 0.66 ms p50 (0.28), an ack's p99 4.1 ms (2.6), upserts 9.8 M rows/s (11.2 M).",
  "memory_mb is the servers' RSS right after the lookups; memory_settled_mb 15 s later. Pondra's allocator (jemalloc, invariant 383) keeps pages freed in the last 10 s for the next query: giving them back after 1 s made TPC-H 4-9% more CPU (L7's ab_compare.py, 16 rounds), so it stays. Pondra 1,978 MB then 463 MB; main 2,137 then 665 MB, the difference being decoded log rows kept after tiering until a read (now dropped when the commit that tiered them arrives). Fluss's three JVMs keep what they hold: 977 then 1,054 MB.",
  "Where Pondra trails: an acknowledged single row (1.6 ms p50 against 1.03), and memory in the moment after the run (2.0 GB against 1.0). Appends are 4.6 M rows/s against 4.7 M, inside either one's run-to-run spread (Pondra 4.34-4.87 M, Fluss 4.54-5.11 M). Where Fluss trails: start (0.08 s against 3.3 s), idle memory (120 MB against 514, one process against three), reading every row back (0.34 s against 0.48), upserts (11 M rows/s against 0.30 M), lookups (0.28 ms p50 against 102 ms), a row seen after its write (2.4 ms against 5.4), grouping (0.10 s against 0.51), memory 15 s after the run (463 MB against 1,054), and its lost upserts at the default batch size."
 ]
}